    {
      "id": "n20-divisibility-certificates",
      "source_numbers": ["312"],
      "view": {
        "mode": "multiple",
        "stem": "Consider these four claims:\n\n$500\\mid(327^3+173^3)$;\n\n$120\\mid(731^3-611^3)$;\n\n$1980\\mid(4759^3-2779^3)$;\n\n$1200\\mid(386^3+214^3)$.\n\nSelect every valid equality that proves one of these claims by expressing its left-hand side as the stated divisor times an integer.",
        "figure_ids": [],
        "options": [
          {"id": "A", "text": "$327^3+173^3=500(327^2+327\\cdot173+173^2)$.", "figure_ids": []},
          {"id": "B", "text": "$731^3-611^3=120(731^2+731\\cdot611+611^2)$.", "figure_ids": []},
          {"id": "C", "text": "$386^3+214^3=1200\\cdot56094$.", "figure_ids": []},
          {"id": "D", "text": "$4759^3-2779^3=1980(4759^2+4759\\cdot2779+2779^2)$.", "figure_ids": []},
          {"id": "E", "text": "$386^3+214^3=1200(386^2-386\\cdot214+214^2)$.", "figure_ids": []},
          {"id": "F", "text": "$327^3+173^3=500(327^2-327\\cdot173+173^2)$.", "figure_ids": []}
        ]
      },
      "intent": {
        "task": "Judge complete divisibility proof certificates for all four original numerical claims, including the extra factor needed for 1200.",
        "source_solution": "327+173=500 gives quotient 80287. 731-611=120 gives quotient 1354323. 4759-2779=1980 gives quotient 43596183. For the last sum, 386+214=600 and the quadratic factor is 112188=2*56094, so the result is 1200*56094.",
        "unit_reason": "One multi-select proof-certificate collection preserves the four divisibility distinctions without separate yes/no questions or combination codes.",
        "concept_blocks": [7, 10],
        "limitations": ["Selecting supplied integer-factor certificates does not demonstrate independently discovering or writing any of the four proofs."],
        "figure_observations": []
      },
      "key": {
        "correct": ["B", "C", "D", "F"],
        "rationales": [
          {"option_id": "A", "truth": "incorrect", "derivation": "A cube sum with factor 327+173 requires the negative middle product. The displayed right side exceeds the left by 1000*327*173.", "misconception": "Use the difference-of-cubes trinomial with the sum first factor."},
          {"option_id": "B", "truth": "correct", "derivation": "731-611=120; the difference-of-cubes quadratic is an integer, yielding the required divisor.", "misconception": null},
          {"option_id": "C", "truth": "correct", "derivation": "The sum is 600*(148996-82604+45796)=600*112188=1200*56094.", "misconception": null},
          {"option_id": "D", "truth": "correct", "derivation": "4759-2779=1980; the exact difference-of-cubes identity supplies an integer quotient.", "misconception": null},
          {"option_id": "E", "truth": "incorrect", "derivation": "The first factor from the cube sum is 600, so this displayed right side is twice the actual value.", "misconception": "Replace the actual sum 386+214=600 with the target divisor 1200 without dividing the remaining factor by 2."},
          {"option_id": "F", "truth": "correct", "derivation": "327+173=500 and the sum-of-cubes second factor is an integer.", "misconception": null}
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "n20-efficient-radicals",
      "source_numbers": ["313"],
      "view": {
        "mode": "single",
        "stem": "Evaluate the principal square roots exactly:\n\n$U=\\sqrt{\\frac{59^3-41^3}{18}+59\\cdot41}$\n\n$V=\\sqrt{\\frac{67^3+52^3}{119}-67\\cdot52}$\n\nWhich ordered pair $(U,V)$ is correct?",
        "figure_ids": [],
        "options": [
          {"id": "A", "text": "$(\\sqrt{7581},\\sqrt{3709})$", "figure_ids": []},
          {"id": "B", "text": "$(100,15)$", "figure_ids": []},
          {"id": "C", "text": "$(18,119)$", "figure_ids": []},
          {"id": "D", "text": "$(100,-15)$", "figure_ids": []}
        ]
      },
      "intent": {
        "task": "Compute both original radical expressions using cube-factor cancellation and perfect-square recognition.",
        "source_solution": "Since 59-41=18, the first radicand becomes 59^2+2*59*41+41^2=(59+41)^2. Since 67+52=119, the second becomes 67^2-2*67*52+52^2=(67-52)^2. Principal roots give 100 and 15.",
        "unit_reason": "The paired computation contrasts the same cube-factor strategy followed by opposite perfect-square signs.",
        "concept_blocks": [7, 10],
        "limitations": ["The selected exact values do not establish that the learner independently used the most efficient method."],
        "figure_observations": []
      },
      "key": {
        "correct": ["B"],
        "rationales": [
          {"option_id": "A", "truth": "incorrect", "derivation": "7581=59^2+59*41+41^2 and 3709=67^2-67*52+52^2 are only the quotient parts of the original radicands.", "misconception": "Factor and cancel the cubes, then omit the outside +59*41 and -67*52 terms."},
          {"option_id": "B", "truth": "correct", "derivation": "The completed square radicands are 10000 and 225, with principal roots 100 and 15.", "misconception": null},
          {"option_id": "C", "truth": "incorrect", "derivation": "These values equal 59-41 and 67+52, reversing the actual perfect-square signs.", "misconception": "Change the first completed square from (59+41)^2 to (59-41)^2 and the second from (67-52)^2 to (67+52)^2."},
          {"option_id": "D", "truth": "incorrect", "derivation": "Although both signs solve z^2=225, the principal square root itself is nonnegative.", "misconception": "Choose the negative solution -15 of z^2=225 as the principal square root."}
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "n20-reduce-domain-table",
      "source_numbers": ["314"],
      "view": {
